[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: Does there exist a nonabelian finite simple group $S$ such that [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$H^2(S, \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})$ is nontrivial, where $p$ is an odd prime?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHere $H^2(S, \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})$ is the second cohomology group (Schur [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmultiplier with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. The Schur multiplier $M(S) = H^2(S, \mathbb{C}^*)$ of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinite simple groups is well-studied. The relationship between $H^2(S, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})$ and the Schur multiplier involves the universal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoefficient theorem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe universal coefficient theorem for cohomology gives:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$H^2(S, \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}) \cong \text{Hom}(H_2(S, \mathbb{Z}), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}) \oplus \text{Ext}^1(H_1(S, \mathbb{Z}), \mathbb{Z}/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp\mathbb{Z})$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $S$ is a nonabelian finite simple group, $H_1(S, \mathbb{Z}) = S^{ab} = 0$[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(the abelianization is trivial). So the Ext term vanishes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThus $H^2(S, \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}) \cong \text{Hom}(H_2(S, \mathbb{Z}), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z})$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow $H_2(S, \mathbb{Z})$ is the Schur multiplier $M(S)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $H^2(S, \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}) \cong \text{Hom}(M(S), \mathbb{Z}/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp\mathbb{Z})$.[0m

